# {{BRAND}} ({{DOMAIN}}): instructions for Claude

{{BRAND}} is an Amazon Associates review site: catalog-driven product pages
with fresh prices, buyer-intent guides, and an agent fleet on the
reusable-agents framework. It is built on the **affiliate-site kit**
(`vendor/affiliate-kit`, kit {{KIT_VERSION}}), which holds the money mechanics
(price rule, CTAs, Offer JSON-LD, click logging), the licence notices, the
deploy gate and the deploy scripts. This repository holds the niche: its
`site.json`, its renderers and domain code, its migrations and content, and
config-only agent directories.

Repository: `{{SITE_DIR}}`, branch `main`. The framework rules in
`reusable-agents/CLAUDE.md` (AgentBase, framework-first, Opus-only authoring,
local-only image generation, pricing integrity) apply here too; this file adds
what is specific to a kit site.

## North star: verified-human Amazon clicks

The one number this site exists to move is **verified-human Amazon clicks**:
clicks on our Amazon links that the click beacon records (`outbound_clicks`,
`via='beacon'`) and that pass the framework's human-click verdict (view
`outbound_clicks_verified_human`), per week, per money placement
(`data-cta-placement`). Organic clicks, indexed pages and IP-verified
assistant fetches (`ai_traffic_log`, `ip_verified IS NOT FALSE`) are the
leading indicators. Pages generated, articles published and code shipped are
not success.

Before any change: name the goal it moves and how the click series will
show it. A change that moves no metric did not happen. Never trade a licence
rule or a gate for clicks.

## The kit boundary (read-only)

- `vendor/affiliate-kit/` is vendored **read-only** by reusable-agents
  `install/sync-site-kit.sh`, which writes `KIT_VERSION` and
  `MANIFEST.sha256`. Before every run the deploy gate
  (`tests/run-local-suite.sh`) checks the vendored copy against that
  manifest, then against the kit version it claims in the reusable-agents
  checkout (`install/sync-site-kit.sh --verify-anchored`: byte-identical to
  that version at a commit there, or in its working tree), and stops (exit
  78) on any difference: an edit, an addition, a removal, a symbolic link,
  or an edit whose manifest line was rewritten to match.
  `tests/unit/kit-integrity.test.ts` runs the same checks in `npm test`.
- What those checks cannot stop: they live in this repository. A session
  that also rewrites the gate shim or that test, or claims a kit version no
  reusable-agents commit holds yet (the anchored check only warns then),
  gets an edited kit through. On the implementer's claude path nothing
  applies the path scope, so there the kit boundary is kept by you alone.
  Never "fix" a check, the manifest, `KIT_VERSION` or a vendored file.
- A kit change is made in reusable-agents `kits/affiliate-site`, released with
  a version bump and a CHANGELOG entry, committed there (the gate anchors the
  vendored copy to reusable-agents commits), then synced:
  `bash /home/voidsstr/development/reusable-agents/install/sync-site-kit.sh {{SITE_DIR}}`
  (the kit README's "Upgrading a site" lists the follow-up steps).
- Import the kit from `vendor/affiliate-kit/server/src` (core),
  `.../server/src/http` (mountKit, beacon, robots, llms), `.../server/src/render`
  (layout, SEO, JSON-LD, Markdown, sitemaps) and `.../server/src/db`
  (migrations). Never copy kit code into `src/`: extend it through
  `site.json`, or change the kit.

## Operator-owned files

Agents never edit these. The implementer scope (`agents/implementer-scope.yaml`)
excludes them for every dispatch kind, so a rec that targets one is deferred
and a stray edit is reverted. That scope is checked on the implementer's
framework-chain path and on every deploy-repair commit, not on its claude
path (its default): there, these rules are yours to keep, and nothing else
stops an edit to `site.json`, the niche policy or `tests/ui/gate.config.ts`
from shipping (`composeGateConfig` refuses a waiver only when the page
manifest sets it). Until reusable-agents enforces the scope on that path,
or its deployer refuses a release that touches these files, the operator
keeps this site's implementer dispatches disabled (`agents/README.md`).

- `site.json`: tracking IDs, segment tags, disclosure, byline, theme, crawler
  policy, IndexNow key, cache and price-freshness limits.
- `{{POLICY_FILE}}`: the niche policy.
- `tests/ui/gate.config.ts`: the gate's policy. The recall and content-policy
  sources (or the reason a site has none), the other compliance sources, the
  product-page specs' sources, and every waiver: a check a family skips, a
  family whose fixture may be empty, a sitemap empty on the fixture, a
  production-only link target. It refuses a page manifest that sets any of
  these, so the gate fails if one is added in the wrong file.
- `CLAUDE.md`, `agents/**`, `prompts/**`, `keep-the-lights-on.yaml`,
  `deploy.env`, `db/fixtures/**`, the gate wiring (`tests/run-local-suite.sh`,
  `tests/suite.env`, `tests/playwright.config.ts`), the deploy files and
  `vendor/**`.

`tests/ui/pages.manifest.ts` (the page families) is the site's own: a change
that adds a page route adds its family there. A deploy repair never edits it
(reusable-agents `deploy_repair` protects every manifest under `tests/`).

## Niche safety boundary (operator-owned)

Fill this in before the first article ships: what this site never
recommends, never claims and never shows. The machine-enforced parts live in
`{{POLICY_FILE}}` and in the content-policy rule sets every rendered page and
article must pass (`tests/ui/gate.config.ts` `CONTENT_RULE_SETS`: {{CONTENT_RULE_SETS_TEXT}}).

## Amazon data rules (Associates licence; the kit and the gate enforce them)

1. **Store only ASINs past 24 hours.** Every value from the Creators API
   (title, image URL, detail-page URL, price, list price, availability, and
   every other API field) is purged within 24h of being fetched; only the ASIN
   and our own derived data (lane, lifecycle, outcome) persist. The purge runs
   only after a successful refresh, and a failed refresh never blanks data.
2. **Prices only through the kit.** `priceStamp()` shows a price only while it
   is quotable (USD, at least $1, fetched within 24h minus the HTML cache age),
   with its UTC time and the exact IP License 2(i) disclaimer behind
   "Details". Never format a price yourself, and never put one in a title, a
   meta or social description, llms.txt or anywhere without a stamp.
3. **The 2(k) notice wherever Amazon text shows.** Wrap Amazon-sourced text in
   `amazonTextHtml()` (or mark it `data-amazon-content`); the layout then
   carries the notice in plain view.
4. **Amazon links only through the kit**: `cta()`, `amazonSearchCta()`,
   `detailLink()` or `/r/<ASIN>`, which set `rel="sponsored nofollow
   noopener"`, `target="_blank"`, a `data-cta-placement` and a tag from
   `site.json`.
5. **No Amazon images stored or reused.** Never in `hero_image_url` or
   `og:image` (a database CHECK and the gate refuse them). Article heroes come
   from the local image daemon (`http://127.0.0.1:7861/generate`) or stay NULL.
6. **No price history, price alerts, price tracking or "lowest ever" /
   "all-time low" claims** (Participation Requirements 6(y)).
7. **HTML caching stays within `site.json` `cache`**: `s-maxage` at most 600,
   never "cache everything" for HTML at a CDN. The price window depends on it.
8. **Pricing integrity**: never crown a pick with an invalid price
   (reusable-agents CLAUDE.md, "Pricing integrity").

## The gate contract

- **The deploy gate** is `bash tests/run-local-suite.sh`: the kit runner with
  `tests/suite.env`. It builds the production image (`Dockerfile.azure`) from
  the tree, starts it on :{{GATE_PORT}} against the fixture Postgres on
  :{{FIXTURE_DB_PORT}}, seeds and re-stamps the fixture (`db/fixtures`), and runs
  the kit's specs (all-pages, kit-compliance, AI fetchers, unbuyable PDPs, CTA
  placements) plus this site's `tests/ui/*.spec.ts`. Before any of that it
  checks the vendored kit against `MANIFEST.sha256` and against the kit
  release it claims in reusable-agents. It never overlaps another
  gate on this host: for the whole run it holds the locks of the gate projects
  `tests/suite.env` names (`SUITE_HOST_LOCKS='{{HOST_LOCKS}}'`; a named lock is
  created when missing, so the exclusion holds even before that gate has run
  since a reboot), and `*` adds any other gate lock present when it starts.
  It runs niced, with a CPU cap on the app and the fixture database.
  Exit codes: 0 pass, 1 tests failed, 2 fixture DB, 3 image build, 4 start,
  5 exited before healthy, 6 never healthy, 7 lock timeout, 8 fixture SQL
  (seed or re-stamp) failed, 78 settings or an edited vendored kit.
- **Every route has a gate family.** A page route in `src/routes/table.ts`
  without a family in `tests/ui/pages.manifest.ts` (or a non-page reason in
  the table) fails the gate; so does a sitemap the manifest does not list.
- **The recall gate and the content gate are never cut.** Each names its
  source in `tests/ui/gate.config.ts`, or states there, in words, why the
  site has none yet; the gate prints that reason on every run.
- **Never weaken a test to pass**, never edit a kit spec, never grant a
  waiver to get a change through, never deploy around the gate.
  `--skip-tests` is only for an operator rollback.
- **Unit tests**: `npm test` (kit integrity, the app's routes and pages, the
  deploy files). `npm run typecheck` checks the site and the vendored kit.

## Git: commit straight to main

Commit straight to `main`: no feature branches unless the operator asks. Never
push unless asked; this repository has no remote until the operator adds one.

## Layout

| Path | What it is |
|---|---|
| `site.json` | the site's identity, tags, notices, theme and limits (the kit's SiteContext) |
| `src/index.ts` | the server: kit middleware first (mountKit), then the route table, then the 404 |
| `src/routes/table.ts` | every route: renderer, sitemap kind and gate family |
| `src/render/` | page renderers on the kit's layout, SEO, JSON-LD and Markdown helpers |
| `src/db/` | the pool and bounded reads |
| `{{POLICY_FILE}}` | the niche policy (operator-owned) |
| `db/migrations/` | site migrations, applied after the kit's (never edit an applied file) |
| `db/fixtures/` | the gate fixture's seed and re-stamp SQL |
| `content/llms.json` | the llms.txt copy |
| `tests/` | gate wiring, the gate policy (`ui/gate.config.ts`) and page manifest (`ui/pages.manifest.ts`), site specs, unit tests |
| `agents/` | config-only agent directories, the implementer scope, registration |
| `prompts/implementer/` | runbook addenda per dispatch kind |
| `deploy.env`, `Dockerfile.azure`, `azure/`, `aws/` | deployment (kit scripts; `vendor/affiliate-kit/deploy/README.md`) |
| `keep-the-lights-on.yaml` | the on-call config (inactive until registered) |
| `vendor/affiliate-kit/` | the kit, read-only |

## Commands

```bash
npm ci                         # dependencies (the kit's exact pins)
npm run typecheck && npm test  # types (site + vendored kit) and unit tests
npm run build                  # dist/ (server) and dist/client (kit browser bundle)
bash tests/run-local-suite.sh  # the deploy gate (docker)
bash azure/deploy.sh --dry-run # the Azure deploy, printed (vendor/affiliate-kit/deploy/README.md)
```

Articles: on a content-policy site, authors write drafts and the publish
gate (reusable-agents `framework/cli/article_publish_gate.py`) promotes them,
but only once the site's migrations install the kit's publish guard
(`vendor/affiliate-kit/migrations/README.md`, "The publish guard"). The
scaffold does not install it, and `editorial_articles.status` defaults to
`published`: until the guard is in, nothing in the database stops an author's
INSERT from going live as written. Install it before the first article.
Privacy page: update it before adding analytics or any other tracking.
